{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/reference/performance.md at exact 9a9be834f73d93ccd458aaaa93667d99fce25ec5 on request/projection-cost, under promise git:sha1…62bf4cf on request git:sha1…5616ac4\n\nRepublished after the re-review verdict fa76797d. One item stood, and it was the\nright one to stand on.\n\nThe memo key had no red proof, and breaking it changes answers. Collapsing\nsettled[receipt.record.ID] to a single slot, so one pass reuses the first\nreceipt's answer for every receipt, left the whole package green while dropping\nstale and stale_because from artifacts that both main and this branch project as\nstale. That is a successor carrying its receipt's historical staleness projected\nfresh — the one thing the freshness checkpoint exists to refuse.\n\nTestCheckpointAnswersPerReceiptWithinOnePass now holds it, in the shape the review\nprescribed. One log, two merges, one staleness pass, opposite checkpoint answers:\nthe first merge has the promise under its approval withdrawn before the receipt,\nso the merge was published with that news in hand and its successor begins a fresh\nepoch; the second has it withdrawn afterwards, so its successor is stale from\nbirth. The fixture refuses to pass on an inert corpus — it asserts both receipts\nare themselves stale first. Keying the memo to anything but the receipt hands the\nfirst answer to the second, and the test goes red.\n\nThe invariant comment no longer overstates its ground. It previously said all\nthree facts are properties of this code rather than of the caller; the closure\nbound is enforced in internal/kernel, and the comment now says so and says which\nfact that is. It also now states why the key is the receipt, beside the memo that\ndepends on it.\n\nConditions met at this exact head.\n\n1. Order of magnitude on an artifact-heavy log. Read-only fold and projection of\na frozen copy of the /Users/hughpyle/play/dap workroom (7,029 events, 5,091\nartifacts, merge receipts present): 18.10 s before, 2.08 s and 2.09 s after. This\nrepository's own log (23,945 events, 6,610 artifacts, 2,697 commitments): 21.70 s\nbefore, 12.82 s after. Same machine, runs one after the other, frozen copies so no\nconcurrent actor moved the head. The halves differ: the artifact-heavy log spent\nits time in the projection, which a reader waits for after every act, and this\nrepository spends its in the fold, at one complete staleness pass per merge\nreceipt admitted, which a resident pays when it starts.\n\n2. Every projection is unchanged. Both logs' complete projection and vocabulary,\nserialized as JSON, are byte-identical before and after: 25,233,705 bytes and\n52 MB. The reviewer re-derived its own differential at each head on corpora built\nto make the bytes move — 183,168 bytes at the previous head, 186,085 at the one\nbefore this, the latter mixing settled and unsettled receipts in one pass — and\nseparately showed memo-on equals memo-off.\n\n3. pathCovers allocates nothing (testing.AllocsPerRun, 0) and its boundaries are\nnamed: exact match, directory above, trailing slash on either side, a sibling\nsharing a prefix without a separator, and a path below its predecessor.\n\n4. No memo outlives the fact it caches. The interval walks and the live-artifact\nindex are built for one projection and dropped with it, and\nTestProjectionIndexesDoNotOutliveTheirFold appends a retirement after a projection\nand shows the next projection reflects it. The receipt memo's per-pass lifetime\nhas its own red proof, which the reviewer measured: hoisting settled out of\ncomputeStaleness is killed by four receipt-checkpoint tests.\n\n5. Every new guard has a mutation that turns a named test red:\n\n  pathCovers separator: TestSuccessionFollowsOnlyACoveringSuccessor,\n    TestPathCoversBoundariesWithoutAllocating\n  empty-interval fast path widened by one:\n    TestMergeLeftLivePostFrontierRemainsUnaccountedUntilNextReceipt,\n    TestPostReceiptAccountingStopsAtTheSuccessor,\n    TestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessorAndRepeats\n  bound each answer by its own successor: TestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessorAndRepeats\n  resume the interval walk rather than restart it: TestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessorAndRepeats\n  changed-path filter in the interval walk:\n    TestMergeChangedPathsBoundsClaimsAndPostFrontierAccounting,\n    TestIntervalWalkAnswersEachSuccessorAndRepeats\n  live subset of the counted interval: TestPostReceiptAccountingSeparatesCountedFromOwed,\n    TestProjectionIndexesDoNotOutliveTheirFold\n  post-frontier bound on the sibling index:\n    TestMergeChangedPathsProtectsNestedSiblingsUntilNamedCommitmentEnds,\n    TestMergeLeftLiveProtectedSiblingIsNotRetirementDebt,\n    TestProtectedSiblingSurvivesARetiredLaterArtifact\n  the receipt's own successor is not a later artifact:\n    TestMergeLeftLiveProtectedSiblingIsNotRetirementDebt,\n    TestProtectedSiblingSurvivesARetiredLaterArtifact,\n    TestReceiptProtectionBeforeAtAndAfterClosure\n  retired artifacts are not live replacements: TestProtectedSiblingSurvivesARetiredLaterArtifact\n  the memo key is the receipt that asked: TestCheckpointAnswersPerReceiptWithinOnePass\n  the memo's lifetime is one pass: four receipt-checkpoint tests, measured by the reviewer\n  per-target supersession index: TestSupersedingARequestThatHoldsAnApprovedHead,\n    TestAnExplicitAbandonmentOutranksAQualifyingTransfer,\n    TestSupersessionIndexHoldsEachTargetInLogOrder\n  commitment protection is read at all:\n    TestMergeChangedPathsProtectsNestedSiblingsUntilNamedCommitmentEnds,\n    TestMergeLeftLiveProtectedSiblingIsNotRetirementDebt,\n    TestProtectedSiblingSurvivesARetiredLaterArtifact\n\nThree mutations are green, and each is stated rather than claimed. Removing the\nreceipt memo entirely changes no answer, which is the point: it changes speed.\nReading commitment protection with an empty staleness set changes no answer\neither, and the reason is structural — staleness reaches Commitment.Status in two\nplaces, each replacing \"open\" or \"promised\", and all three words are in the active\nset, so the staleness set cannot change membership in either direction. The third\nis the reviewer's own: dating the walk by receipt.index instead of\nreceipt.sequence() is inert, because sequence() is index+1 and activeRetirements\ndates by sequence, so the two differ only for a cause at the receipt's own index,\nwhich cannot exist.\n\n6. docs/reference/performance.md records the measurement, both corpora shapes, the\nfour repeated reads, and what the numbers do not establish.\n\n7. go vet ./... clean and go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./... green at this exact\nhead, exit 0 read from the process rather than through a pipeline, 37 packages, no\nfailures.\n\nArchitecture: unchanged, and the review confirmed it at both previous heads. The\nchange is inside the Workroom application profile's fold and the projection it\nalready produced; no layer contract in docs/reference/architecture.md moves, and\nno fold version bump is owed because the projection bytes a cache is keyed on do\nnot move. That is why the architecture page is not in this artifact set.","body":{"commit":"9a9be834f73d93ccd458aaaa93667d99fce25ec5","path":"docs/reference/performance.md"}}